WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.368 --> 00:00:02.869 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Years ago when they
started this censoring thing

00:00:02.869 --> 00:00:06.006 align:start size:97% position:3%
on Twitter, and on Facebook,
and on Google, and so on.

00:00:06.006 --> 00:00:08.975 align:start size:94% position:6%
I said, "No, no, you
have to make regulations.

00:00:08.975 --> 00:00:10.543
It's a new industry.

00:00:10.543 --> 00:00:13.480
Every human interaction
is regulated by the law"

00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:14.748
And conservatives were going,

00:00:14.748 --> 00:00:16.583
"No, they must have
freedom of speech."

00:00:16.583 --> 00:00:18.184
I thought, "Absolutely not."

00:00:18.184 --> 00:00:21.621 align:start size:84% position:16%
You know, these
guys are basically,

00:00:21.621 --> 00:00:23.189 align:start size:84% position:16%
what do they call
'em, common carriers.

00:00:23.189 --> 00:00:24.724
They're like the phone company.

00:00:24.724 --> 00:00:28.161
If I'm on the phone to you
and the operator comes on

00:00:28.161 --> 00:00:30.330
and says, "Excuse me, sir,
you can't say that to Dave.

00:00:30.330 --> 00:00:31.664 align:start size:84% position:16%
And Dave can't
respond," you know?

00:00:31.664 --> 00:00:33.333
No, that guy gets carried away

00:00:33.333 --> 00:00:35.668 align:start size:88% position:13%
'cause there's a
regulation against that.

00:00:35.668 --> 00:00:37.303
We need to do that now.

00:00:37.303 --> 00:00:38.638
We're just catching up with it.

00:00:38.638 --> 00:00:40.173
I feel it's happening.

00:00:40.173 --> 00:00:42.609
It may happen in the
Supreme Court session.

00:00:42.609 --> 00:00:45.078
Certainly, there's
laws now in Texas,

00:00:45.078 --> 00:00:46.913 align:start size:84% position:16%
that have been
supported by courts

00:00:46.913 --> 00:00:49.949
where the court just said,
in Texas, "No, you know,

00:00:49.949 --> 00:00:52.652
they don't have a right
to violate free speech."

00:00:52.652 --> 00:00:54.287
It's a new thing.

00:00:54.287 --> 00:00:55.989
Almost everything
we're going through

00:00:55.989 --> 00:00:58.058
is traceable back
to the internet,

00:00:58.058 --> 00:01:00.260 align:start position:0%
in the same way that everything
during the reformation

00:01:00.260 --> 00:01:01.895
is traceable back to
the printing press.

00:01:01.895 --> 00:01:04.497
(upbeat music)

00:01:09.002 --> 00:01:10.503
- I'm Dave Rubin,

00:01:10.503 --> 00:01:12.872 align:start size:97% position:3%
and joining me today is the
host of the Andrew Klavan Show

00:01:12.872 --> 00:01:16.209
on The Daily Wire, as well as
author of the brand new book,

00:01:16.209 --> 00:01:18.611
"A Strange Habit of Mind."

00:01:18.611 --> 00:01:21.514
Andrew Klavan, welcome
back to the Ruben Report.

00:01:21.514 --> 00:01:22.982
- Great to see you, Dave.

00:01:22.982 --> 00:01:25.452 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Andrew, you've done many
things in your career.

00:01:25.452 --> 00:01:29.456
Screenwriting, many
books, Daily Wire,

00:01:29.456 --> 00:01:30.957
but you opened for me

00:01:30.957 --> 00:01:32.926
during the "Don't Burn in
This Country" book tour.

00:01:32.926 --> 00:01:36.062
Would you say that
was the highlight of
the whole operation?

00:01:36.062 --> 00:01:38.598
And why even continue
working after that?

00:01:40.033 --> 00:01:42.936 align:start position:0%
- I struggled with that question
after opening for your act.

00:01:42.936 --> 00:01:45.805
I thought, you know, I'm
now so covered in shame

00:01:45.805 --> 00:01:48.374 align:start size:81% position:19%
that maybe there's
no possibility

00:01:48.374 --> 00:01:50.076
of going on with any
self-respect at all.

00:01:50.076 --> 00:01:51.678
But I've managed a little bit.

00:01:51.678 --> 00:01:53.246
- How many books
have you written?

00:01:53.246 --> 00:01:54.848
Every time I bring you on,
I ask you how many books,

00:01:54.848 --> 00:01:55.915
and I'm still not even
sure how many books.

00:01:55.915 --> 00:01:57.884
It's a lot of books.

00:01:57.884 --> 00:01:59.152
- Well, it's a lot of books.

00:01:59.152 --> 00:02:01.321
The last book, when
they published it,

00:02:01.321 --> 00:02:03.556
they said it was my 36th, so,
I'm just gonna go with that.

00:02:03.556 --> 00:02:06.259
- Wow, I mean, that
is really something.

00:02:08.161 --> 00:02:10.430
When you finish a book, do
you feel like it's your last,

00:02:10.430 --> 00:02:13.833
and then suddenly there's
some more in the well,

00:02:13.833 --> 00:02:15.301
or how do you feel usually?

00:02:15.301 --> 00:02:18.138
- No, the, only time I
ever thought of giving up

00:02:18.138 --> 00:02:20.940
was after writing a book
called "Empire of Lies,"

00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:22.775
which was a very difficult book

00:02:22.775 --> 00:02:26.045
and a very emotional book,
and it really wrecked me.

00:02:26.045 --> 00:02:27.981 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I walked in and I said
to my wife, "That's it,

00:02:27.981 --> 00:02:30.049
I'm done writing novels."

00:02:30.049 --> 00:02:32.819
And she said, "Sit down
and have some dinner."

00:02:32.819 --> 00:02:34.420
She didn't even believe me.

00:02:34.420 --> 00:02:35.855 align:start size:88% position:13%
There wasn't even a
second where she thought

00:02:35.855 --> 00:02:37.223 align:start size:81% position:19%
that I might be
telling the truth.

00:02:37.223 --> 00:02:38.591
It's kind of like an addiction.

00:02:38.591 --> 00:02:39.926
I can't stop.

00:02:39.926 --> 00:02:41.594 align:start size:88% position:13%
- All right, so I wanna
get into the book,

00:02:41.594 --> 00:02:43.229
so we just got a
copy this morning.

00:02:43.229 --> 00:02:45.598
So in full disclosure,
I have not read it yet,

00:02:45.598 --> 00:02:47.167
but I've read the synopsis here,

00:02:47.167 --> 00:02:49.602
and obviously between big
tech, and billionaires,

00:02:49.602 --> 00:02:51.371
and espionage, and
all sorts of stuff,

00:02:51.371 --> 00:02:52.639
there's a lot that's relevant

00:02:52.639 --> 00:02:53.940
to our political
stuff of the day.

00:02:53.940 --> 00:02:56.075
So they'll be plenty
for us to talk about.

00:02:56.075 --> 00:03:00.079
But first off, you, like
me and so many others,

00:03:00.079 --> 00:03:02.348
are a California expat.

00:03:02.348 --> 00:03:05.084
You are a refugee
of what is now,

00:03:05.084 --> 00:03:07.353 align:start size:84% position:16%
I would say,
Communist California.

00:03:07.353 --> 00:03:08.955
You're feeling pretty good.

00:03:08.955 --> 00:03:11.324
Do people know publicly where
you live, sort of, roughly?

00:03:11.324 --> 00:03:13.893 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Yeah, I'm in Virginia
in the DC area,

00:03:13.893 --> 00:03:17.263 align:start size:94% position:6%
and yeah, it's the
smartest thing I ever did.

00:03:17.263 --> 00:03:20.767 align:start position:0%
I mean, it was the zombie
apocalypse by the time we left.

00:03:22.001 --> 00:03:23.203
I was living in the
Hollywood Hills,

00:03:23.203 --> 00:03:25.238 align:start size:84% position:16%
which is a very
ritzy neighborhood.

00:03:25.238 --> 00:03:26.639
And by the time I left,

00:03:26.639 --> 00:03:29.142 align:start size:97% position:3%
I was patrolling my
backyard at night with a gun

00:03:29.142 --> 00:03:31.244 align:start size:84% position:16%
to make sure nobody
had moved in.

00:03:31.244 --> 00:03:32.679
You know, I had friends

00:03:32.679 --> 00:03:35.014 align:start size:97% position:3%
who had found homeless
people in their apartments.

00:03:35.014 --> 00:03:36.950 align:start size:84% position:16%
Friends who found them
in their backyard.

00:03:36.950 --> 00:03:38.718 align:start size:97% position:3%
And, you know, it's nothing
against the homeless,

00:03:38.718 --> 00:03:39.986
but it's nothing compassionate

00:03:39.986 --> 00:03:41.554
about letting people
live on the streets.

00:03:41.554 --> 00:03:43.089
It's not compassionate to them.

00:03:43.089 --> 00:03:45.325
And it's not compassionate to
the people who come to a city

00:03:45.325 --> 00:03:47.360 align:start size:84% position:16%
to find a way of life
that's, you know,

00:03:47.360 --> 00:03:48.461
pleasant and uplifting.

00:03:48.461 --> 00:03:49.829
I don't think you can do that

00:03:49.829 --> 00:03:52.065
when your city is
overrun by people.

00:03:52.065 --> 00:03:53.600
Not just homeless people,

00:03:53.600 --> 00:03:57.170
but also criminals who are not
being restrained in any way.

00:03:57.170 --> 00:03:59.072
It's just amazing to me.

00:03:59.072 --> 00:04:03.109
It is absolutely amazing to me
that a state like California,

00:04:03.109 --> 00:04:05.278
which is paradise, I mean,
if you take the people out,

00:04:05.278 --> 00:04:08.381
it's just paradise, can be
destroyed at that level.

00:04:08.381 --> 00:04:11.484
And yet people go on voting for
the people who destroyed it.

00:04:11.484 --> 00:04:14.120
I once asked Larry Elder
why he thought that was so,

00:04:14.120 --> 00:04:15.955
and he said, they
just don't connect

00:04:15.955 --> 00:04:18.658
the policies to the apocalypse.

00:04:18.658 --> 00:04:19.759
It's all policy.

00:04:19.759 --> 00:04:21.861
I mean, New York was like that

00:04:21.861 --> 00:04:23.630
in the '80s and '90s
when I lived there.

00:04:23.630 --> 00:04:26.065
And then Giuliani came
in, changed the policies.

00:04:26.065 --> 00:04:27.867 align:start size:88% position:13%
They called him a
racist every single day

00:04:27.867 --> 00:04:28.801
in the New York Times.

00:04:28.801 --> 00:04:30.069
He cleaned the city up,

00:04:30.069 --> 00:04:32.639
and made it one of the
most beautiful cities,

00:04:32.639 --> 00:04:34.440
one of the safest big
cities in the world,

00:04:34.440 --> 00:04:35.908
if not the safest.

00:04:35.908 --> 00:04:39.479 align:start size:97% position:3%
It took, you know, two years
to destroy all that work

00:04:39.479 --> 00:04:42.115
that was kept in place by
Bloomberg all those years.

00:04:42.115 --> 00:04:44.817
Took just two years of left
wing policies to destroy it.

00:04:44.817 --> 00:04:48.588 align:start position:0%
It's hard to understand why
people will not turn the corner.

00:04:48.588 --> 00:04:51.190 align:start size:94% position:6%
I don't know whether it's
the feeling of virtue

00:04:51.190 --> 00:04:52.925
that leftism gives them,

00:04:52.925 --> 00:04:55.962
but, I'm just so happy
to be out of that place.

00:04:55.962 --> 00:04:58.298 align:start size:97% position:3%
It was absolutely disgusting
by the time I left.

00:04:58.298 --> 00:05:01.534 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Do you see any hope,
because, you know,

00:05:01.534 --> 00:05:04.203 align:start size:84% position:16%
we had a couple
dinners in LA before.

00:05:04.203 --> 00:05:05.805
You left first, wisely,

00:05:05.805 --> 00:05:07.774
and then I followed a
couple months later,

00:05:07.774 --> 00:05:09.676
but all of our friends left.

00:05:09.676 --> 00:05:11.344
And from everything I've seen

00:05:11.344 --> 00:05:14.013
in basically the nine or so
months that I've been gone,

00:05:14.013 --> 00:05:16.482
it's just getting worse.

00:05:16.482 --> 00:05:20.386 align:start size:97% position:3%
I mean, how should we feel,
the people that leave?

00:05:20.386 --> 00:05:22.622 align:start size:97% position:3%
Like should we feel, oh, maybe
they will turn it around?

00:05:22.622 --> 00:05:24.123
Or should we be hopeful?

00:05:24.123 --> 00:05:25.591 align:start size:94% position:6%
Or is it just like, you
know what, enough already,

00:05:25.591 --> 00:05:28.494
that's what you guys want, then
just keep running with that.

00:05:28.494 --> 00:05:29.729
- Well, you know,

00:05:29.729 --> 00:05:31.230
I always hope it is one
of the United States.

00:05:31.230 --> 00:05:34.634 align:start size:97% position:3%
I always hope that it'll
come back, you know, online.

00:05:34.634 --> 00:05:35.902
And in the past,

00:05:35.902 --> 00:05:39.972
because so much of the
industry is creative.

00:05:39.972 --> 00:05:43.042 align:start size:97% position:3%
There's Hollywood and there's,
you know, Silicon Valley.

00:05:43.042 --> 00:05:45.511 align:start size:97% position:3%
So these are people who are
creative, they're capitalists.

00:05:45.511 --> 00:05:46.713 align:start size:88% position:13%
They like to pretend
they're not capitalists.

00:05:46.713 --> 00:05:48.548 align:start size:84% position:16%
- So they can make
speeches at the Oscars

00:05:48.548 --> 00:05:49.882
about not being capitalist,

00:05:49.882 --> 00:05:51.484 align:start position:0%
but they're the most
capitalist people in the world.

00:05:51.484 --> 00:05:53.353 align:start size:88% position:13%
And so when their
business gets undercut,

00:05:53.353 --> 00:05:54.821
to a certain degree,

00:05:54.821 --> 00:05:56.456 align:start size:94% position:6%
you think that they're
gonna come back and elect,

00:05:56.456 --> 00:05:59.125
you know, a Reagan or a, you
know, even a Schwarzenegger,

00:05:59.125 --> 00:06:01.160
somebody to sort of
change things up.

00:06:01.160 --> 00:06:04.063 align:start size:94% position:6%
The problem now is that
because we live in a world

00:06:04.063 --> 00:06:05.665 align:start size:84% position:16%
where we can be
connected like this,

00:06:05.665 --> 00:06:07.700 align:start size:97% position:3%
like you and I are
connected right this minute,

00:06:07.700 --> 00:06:10.236 align:start size:88% position:13%
there's not that much
difference if you leave,

00:06:10.236 --> 00:06:11.704
you can build an
industry anywhere.

00:06:11.704 --> 00:06:13.406 align:start size:88% position:13%
You can run an
industry from anywhere.

00:06:13.406 --> 00:06:17.076 align:start size:97% position:3%
And so my concern is that
now people will simply move,

00:06:17.076 --> 00:06:18.544
you know, to Nashville.

00:06:18.544 --> 00:06:20.913 align:start size:97% position:3%
If you go to Nashville,
everybody there is from LA,

00:06:20.913 --> 00:06:23.116 align:start size:97% position:3%
you know, people will
simply move to other places

00:06:23.116 --> 00:06:25.551 align:start size:84% position:16%
and just let the place
sink into the sea.

00:06:25.551 --> 00:06:29.756 align:start size:88% position:13%
You think eventually
the absolute destruction

00:06:29.756 --> 00:06:33.893
of a brilliant economy,
will back up on them.

00:06:33.893 --> 00:06:35.561
You know, it just,
you think that people

00:06:35.561 --> 00:06:36.929
will only stand it so long,

00:06:36.929 --> 00:06:39.332
and yet the fact that
they have stood by,

00:06:39.332 --> 00:06:41.033 align:start size:84% position:16%
well, for instance,
San Francisco,

00:06:41.033 --> 00:06:42.468 align:start size:94% position:6%
one of the most beautiful
cities on earth,

00:06:42.468 --> 00:06:45.037 align:start size:97% position:3%
has been turned into a
hell hole, is just amazing.

00:06:45.037 --> 00:06:46.239
It is just amazing.

00:06:46.239 --> 00:06:47.673
So I don't know if
it's gonna come back.

00:06:47.673 --> 00:06:48.908 align:start size:94% position:6%
- It's interesting because
more and more people

00:06:48.908 --> 00:06:51.611 align:start size:84% position:16%
are talking about what
is the united part

00:06:51.611 --> 00:06:53.279
of the United States of America.

00:06:53.279 --> 00:06:55.081 align:start size:84% position:16%
You know, it's like,
I keep saying it,

00:06:55.081 --> 00:06:57.917
but I honestly feel like I
moved to a different country,

00:06:57.917 --> 00:07:00.920
not to a different state,
but to a different country.

00:07:00.920 --> 00:07:03.956 align:start size:97% position:3%
And that I guess that
leaves us with the question

00:07:03.956 --> 00:07:05.491
of what makes us united.

00:07:05.491 --> 00:07:08.060 align:start size:97% position:3%
And I think there's a lot
of, you know, conservatives,

00:07:08.060 --> 00:07:11.364
or whatever you want to call
broadly people on the right,

00:07:11.364 --> 00:07:13.232
that are just like,
there's nothing left.

00:07:13.232 --> 00:07:16.002 align:start size:84% position:16%
There's nothing
left, and that's it.

00:07:16.002 --> 00:07:18.237 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Well, that is a thing
conservatives do, you know,

00:07:18.237 --> 00:07:20.406
that's kind of a
knee-jerk thing.

00:07:20.406 --> 00:07:22.675
Conservatives, "It's over,
it's all over, I give up,"

00:07:22.675 --> 00:07:25.478 align:start position:0%
and all this, and you know,
when Roe v Wade was overturned,

00:07:25.478 --> 00:07:28.314
I just reminded people, you
know, that things can change.

00:07:28.314 --> 00:07:29.615
Amazing work can be done.

00:07:29.615 --> 00:07:31.117
It takes a long time.

00:07:31.117 --> 00:07:33.553
It took the left 60 years to
take over our institutions

00:07:33.553 --> 00:07:35.254
and bring the country this low.

00:07:35.254 --> 00:07:37.256
It's gonna be a long
fight to take it back,

00:07:37.256 --> 00:07:38.791
but it can be done.

00:07:38.791 --> 00:07:41.961 align:start size:88% position:13%
My hope is that we
become more federalists.

00:07:41.961 --> 00:07:44.831
We let the states
interdependently

00:07:44.831 --> 00:07:46.399 align:start size:84% position:16%
become laboratories
of democracy,

00:07:46.399 --> 00:07:49.202
try different things,
and that as we do that,

00:07:49.202 --> 00:07:51.103
the good ideas will spread.

00:07:51.103 --> 00:07:53.606 align:start size:84% position:16%
The alternative
scenario to that is

00:07:53.606 --> 00:07:55.541 align:start size:97% position:3%
we become more federalists,
we split apart,

00:07:55.541 --> 00:07:58.478 align:start position:0%
and eventually we find ourselves
at each other's throats.

00:07:58.478 --> 00:08:00.046
But I hope that's
not gonna happen.

00:08:00.046 --> 00:08:01.747 align:start size:94% position:6%
In fact, one of the things
that I hope will help

00:08:01.747 --> 00:08:03.850
is the repeal of Roe v. Wade.

00:08:03.850 --> 00:08:05.585
I hope that because of that,

00:08:05.585 --> 00:08:09.422 align:start size:97% position:3%
cultures are going to form
that attract and repel people.

00:08:09.422 --> 00:08:11.791
And I think that, you know,
right now it's the economy.

00:08:11.791 --> 00:08:13.259
People go places for the jobs,

00:08:13.259 --> 00:08:15.127
they go places to get
away from the homeless,

00:08:15.127 --> 00:08:16.329
and from the crime.

00:08:16.329 --> 00:08:17.663 align:start size:84% position:16%
But soon people
are gonna go places

00:08:17.663 --> 00:08:19.765 align:start size:88% position:13%
to live with other
people like themselves.

00:08:19.765 --> 00:08:24.170
And as one way of living
works better than others,

00:08:24.170 --> 00:08:27.139
I think maybe more states
will turn over, we'll see.

00:08:27.139 --> 00:08:29.175
- You know, I should tell
you that here in Miami,

00:08:29.175 --> 00:08:33.513
I did finally see one person
with a Charlie Crist sign,

00:08:33.513 --> 00:08:35.047 align:start size:84% position:16%
and I almost knocked
on their door

00:08:35.047 --> 00:08:37.850
and offered them free mental
health services for a year.

00:08:37.850 --> 00:08:39.352
It's like, what are you doing?

00:08:39.352 --> 00:08:41.087
You're here in heaven.

00:08:41.087 --> 00:08:42.154
And you've got

00:08:42.154 --> 00:08:43.356 align:start size:84% position:16%
a Charlie Crist sign.
- I know.

00:08:43.356 --> 00:08:44.590
Yeah, that's the big fear,

00:08:44.590 --> 00:08:45.858
that they'll bring
California with 'em.

00:08:45.858 --> 00:08:47.426
- So we're taping this

00:08:47.426 --> 00:08:49.729 align:start size:84% position:16%
just a little bit
before these midterms.

00:08:49.729 --> 00:08:52.064 align:start size:97% position:3%
I mean, are you basically
feeling like this, you know,

